      "confidence": "low",
      "note": "Best effort: hours as the feminine ordinal 'tis' + numeral ('tis snat' 2:00, 'tis yat' 1:00, 'tis snat d mrawt' 12:xx), 'tis snat d uzgen' (2:30), 'tis snat d rrbɛ' (2:15), 'tis snat d mraw tusdiqin' (2:10), 20+ minutes with the everyday Arabic-loan numerals and 'n' + singular ('d ɛcrin n tusdiqt', 'd xmsa u ṛbɛin n tusdiqt'). Doubts: whether 'tis yat' is used for one o'clock (vs 'tamzwarut'); whether speakers prefer a subtractive 'quarter to' form (not attempted); the locale's minute word 'tusdiqt' (IRCAM uses tusdidt); in practice many Moroccan Amazigh speakers tell time in Darija. For tzm the same phrases were used; Central Atlas forms may differ. noon/midnight transliterated to Latin (tizwarnin = midday prayer time, nṣ n iḍ = half of night)."
